
$theme {}

$title { Notes on the Lyapunov Exponent }

$comment { $og_image { /media/lyapunov/preview.png } }

$og_description { }

$css {
    .interactive-plot {
        width: 100%;
        height: 500px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {

        .interactive-plot {
            height: 85vh;
            border-bottom: 3px double var(--main-solid);
            border-top: 3px double var(--main-solid);
        }
    }

    .gallery img:not(.loaded) {
        filter: url(#thumb-blur);
        clip-path: inset(0.25rem);
    }

    #lightbox {
        display: none;
        position: fixed;
        inset: 0;
        background: #000a;
        place-items: center;
        z-index: 2;
    }

    #lightbox.open {
        display: grid;
    }

    #lightbox img {
        max-width: 98vw;
        max-height: 98vh;
        object-fit: contain;
    }

}

#lightbox[onclick=this.classList.remove('open')] {
    img#lightbox-img {}
}

svg[style=height:0;width:0;margin:0;padding:0;display:block] {
  filter#thumb-blur {
    feGaussianBlur[stdDeviation=3 | edgeMode=duplicate]{}
  }
}

article{

hgroup {
    h1 { Notes on Lyapunov Exponents }

    hr {}
}



noscript.info-box {
    It looks like you have scripts turned off. Just so you know, there's an interactive WebGPU-based fractal explorer you're missing out on, and a few other interactive visualizations throughout the article where you'll see a handful of screenshots instead.
}







section{

hgroup{
    h2#section_ii {
                                § II. Understanding the Lyapunov Exponent
    }
    a[href=#contents]{ ↩ contents }
}

Behold the Lyapunov exponent:

$math/math { block lambda = underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|d/{d x} f_i{(x_i)}|} }

It's my intent that by the end of this section most readers will find this formula to be a perfectly straightforward answer to a perfectly natural question; but to start our journey it's helpful to take a look at the kind of system the Lyapunov exponent is meant to help us understand. One such example, the function $math/math { f{(x)} = r dot x dot {(1 - x)}}, is known as the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_map]{logistic map}. Let's take a look at how this function behaves across the unit interval $math/math { [0,1]}:

.dynamic.interactive-plot { $math/plot { logistic_3d } }

aside.dynamic.info-box { In interactive plots, the bounds of the sliders have been set somewhat conservatively, for the sake of readers who may be using low-end devices. However, most of these limits are not enforced for the textual input elements. You may push your device to its limits, at your own peril. }

noscript.info-box {
    TODO add a gallery of screenshots showcasing the plots
}

Playing around with the parameters a bit, one can observe a number of basic facts:

ul {
li { For r from 0 to 4, the unit interval is mapped back into itself, so no matter how many times we apply the map, we don't have to worry about anything outside that range. }
li { Since $math/math { f(0) = 0 } and $math/math { f(1) = 0 }, those make for uninteresting starting values. }
li { For small values of r, the unit interval (excluding the endpoints) converges on a single value. }
li { As r increases, at some point there's a change, and each point on the interval eventually finds itself bouncing between two values, but when that happens those are the em{same} two values, across the whole interval. }
li { As r increases further, there are more values to bounce between, and by the time r is quite close to 4, the plot gets very messy very quickly and this ceases to be a useful visualization. }
}

The most important takeaway from that visualization is that, as long as it's not 0 or 1, we could start from $noted{ almost any $note There do exist other exceptional initial conditions. Try out $math/math { x_0 = 0.75, r = 4 }, for instance. The set of periodic initial conditions in otherwise-chaotic parametrizations is an interesting object of study that merits a mention even if it's outside the scope of this post. } x-value and the system's long-term dynamics would be qualitatively similar. So, let's not keep wasting a dimension of our visualization on that. Instead, let's take a i{single} $math/math { x_0 } and plot its behavior for many timesteps:

.dynamic.interactive-plot { $math/plot { logistic/timeseries } }

noscript.info-box {
    TODO add a gallery of screenshots showcasing the plots
}

We can see here that indeed the long-term qualitative behavior of the system is not greatly impacted by our choice of $math/math { x_0 }. We can see more clearly now the periodic and aperiodic. If you turn up the iteration count, you'll see that plot becomes qualitatively monotonous in the long run in either case. So, plotting the entire timeseries is still a bit of a waste of a dimension in our visualization of this system. Perhaps we should sweep across values of $math/math { r} and view some kind of summary of the long-term behavior of the system under that parametrization.

todo: the classic logistic histogram sweep :)

Now we're getting somewhere. There is a lot going on here. This kind of figure is known as a bifurcation diagram, and the bifurcation diagram of the logistic map has been studied i{extensively}. There's a good chance you've seen it before; maybe you've even seen a demonstration of how it relates to the Mandelbrot set. It is tempting to attempt to catalogue every interesting fact and feature of the bifurcation diagram, but this is an article about em{Lyapunov fractals}, so let's stay on task.

In the context of our journey toward understanding the Lyapunov fractal, we are interested in a single simple question about each parametrization: is the trajectory stable, or chaotic? In a sense, the bifurcation diagram is telling us em{too much}. We'd like to put a single number to each parametrization, characterizing just how tidy or untidy a system it defined. We can look at any slice of the bifurcation diagram and eyeball it, but how can we em{compute} it?

Well, one idea would be to simply take the limit of the average of the logarithm of the magnitude of the derivative:


:)

Many of the explanations you can find online of the Lyapunov fractal would be happy to just drop this formula on you from on high and move on, but personally I think you'd be justified in demanding a little more explanation than that.
$math/math { auto lambda = underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|d/{d x} f_i{(x_i)}|} }
The idea from which this measure (the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_exponent]{maximal Lyapunov exponent, or abbr{MLE}}) arises is that of defining the quantity we're after as em{sensitivity to initial conditions}. If we give the system a tiny nudge at the start, how much do we expect that to change the result, hundreds of iterations later? If you return to the previous visualizations and toy with them for a bit, you can see that while the long term behavior of the system is i{qualitatively} largely independent of $math/math { x_0}, this idea of how sensitive the i{specific values} at any point late in the sequence are to slight changes in $math/math { x_0} lines up well with our looser intuitive notions of apparent-order-versus-apparent-chaos.

So then we just need to quantify this sensitivity. Let's consider just one iteration. How sensitive is $math/math { x_1 = f(x_0) } to a change in $math/math { x_0 }? Well, this is exactly what the differentiation operator $math/math{ d/{d x}} is for. Speaking a bit loosely, we denote "the rate at which $math/math{ b} changes, proportionate to a tiny change in $math/math{ a}" as $math/math { d/{d a} b}. Since the quantity we are after is the change in $math/math { x_n} induced by a tiny change in $math/math { x_0}, what we are looking for is $math/math { d/{d x_0} x_n}.

Following a common convention, from here on we will denote the derivative of a function with a tick mark, like so: $math/math { f' defineeq d/{d x} f }. The logistic map is a polynomial function, and there is a well known straightforward procedure for obtaining the derivatives of polynomials. As we are interested only in this one function, we won't be diving into the methodological details. For $math/math { f(x) = r dot x dot (1 - x) }, the derivative is $math/math { f'{(x)} = r - 2 dot r dot x}

Let's start with the case of $math/math { x_1 }. We have $math/math { d/{d x_0} x_1 = f'{(x_0)}}.

Now, how about $math/math { x_2 }? Well, we have $math/math { x_2 = f{(x_1)} = f(f(x_0)) } and thus $math/math { d/{d x_0} x_2 = d/{d x_0} f{(x_1)} = d/{d x_0} f{(f{(x_0)})} }. We must be more careful than simply replacing $math/math { f } with $math/math { f' } in a nested case like this, and instead apply something called the a[href=]{chain rule}:

$math/math { block d/{d x} a{(b{(x)})} = a'{(b{(x)})} dot b'{(x)} }

In our case, this evaluates to:

$math/math { block d/{d x_0} f{(f{(x_0)})} = f'{(f{(x_0)})} dot f'{(x_0)} = f'{(x_1)} dot f'{(x_0)} }

It is left as an exercise for the reader to prove that the pattern holds:

$math/math { block d/{d x_0} x_n = underover product {i=0} {n-1} end f'{(x_i)} defineeq f'{(x_{n-1})} dot f'{(x_{n-2})} dot ... dot f'{(x_1)} dot f'{(x_0)} }

Now, we are interested in whether and to what degree this quantity em{grows} or em{shrinks} as the number of iterations increases. There happens to be an exceedingly useful mathematical tool for modeling growth and decay, known as $noted { the a[href=]{exponential function}, denoted as either $note When $math/math { x } is a real number, applying the exponential function to it and taking $math/math { e } to the $math/math { x^{th} } power are precisely the same thing. When it is not a real number, $math/math { e^x } is a frequently used shorthand for $math/math { exp{(x)} } which some, including the author of this post, regard as an abuse of notation. } $math/math { exp{(x)} } or $math/math { e^x } . The exponential of a negative value tends toward zero, while the exponential of a positive value grows.

Since the exponential of a real value is always positive, we will have to take the absolute value of our derivative if we want to model it as an exponential. This is fine, as what we really care about is the magnitude. Calling the rate of exponential growth or decay $math/math { lambda_n } at iteration $math/math { n }, we have:

$math/math { block e^{n dot lambda_n} = d/{d x_0} x_n = underover product {i=0} {n-1} end {|f'{(x_i)}|} }

Now, since the (natural) logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we can take the log of both sides and obtain:

$math/math { block lambda_n = 1/n log underover product {i=0} {n-1} end {|f'{(x_i)}|} }

(It is worth noting that the original Markus paper actually uses a base-2 logarithm; this is equivalent to simply applying a constant scaling factor.) Now, this is already a nice formula for $math/math { lambda_n }, but if we try to do actual computation with it, the product will be numerically unstable. However it is a well-known and very useful algebraic property of logarithms that the logarithm of a product is a sum of logarithms:

$math/math { block lambda_n = 1/n underover sum {i=0} {n-1} end log {|f'{(x_i)}|} }

Now if we just ask "what does this $math/math { lambda_n } tend toward as $math/math { n } grows?", we have recovered our formula from earlier:

$math/math { block lambda = underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=m} {n-1} end log{|f'{(x_i)}|} }

You may have noticed that I snuck a variable $math/math { m } in there in place of zero. In practice it is convenient to skip the first several iterations when computing this value, as the system can take a little while to settle into its limiting behavior, and there are some pesky issues with the very first iteration in particular (more on this in the subsection on "Algebraic Curves" in section IV).

So, now that we've derived the Lyapunov exponent, let's plot it:

$footnotes { Section II Footnotes }

}

